        <p>Every Sunday, someone pulls on our door at MLK and Chicon, reads the sign, and sighs. We get it. Texas liquor law is a patchwork of rules that made sense to someone in 1935 and still shapes when and how you can buy a bottle today. Here's the plain-English version from a family that lives by these rules six days a week.</p>

        <h2>Why Are Liquor Stores Closed on Sunday in Texas?</h2>
        <p>Because state law says so. Texas package stores &mdash; the legal name for a liquor store &mdash; are only permitted to sell distilled spirits Monday through Saturday. Sunday sales of liquor are prohibited statewide, no matter the city or county. This is a holdover from the "blue laws" that once restricted all kinds of Sunday commerce; most of those rules have been repealed over the decades, but the Sunday liquor ban has stuck around.</p>
        <p>So it's not that we're taking the day off. Both <a href="/east-austin.html">our East Austin store</a> and <a href="/lakeway.html">our Lakeway store</a> are closed Sundays because we're required to be. Every liquor store in Texas is in the same boat.</p>

        <h2>The 10am to 9pm Window</h2>
        <p>Monday through Saturday, package stores may sell from 10am to 9pm. That's the ceiling set by the state, and it's the schedule we keep at both locations. A few practical notes:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Doors open at 10am sharp, not before. If you're waiting outside at 9:55, we'll wave, but we can't ring you up.</li>
          <li>Sales stop at 9pm. Plan your Saturday night run with a little cushion.</li>
          <li>The same window applies to online orders. Delivery and curbside pickup happen inside the hours the store is legally open.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Holiday Closures (and the Following-Monday Rule)</h2>
        <p>Texas liquor stores must close on three holidays each year: Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Day, and New Year's Day. There's a wrinkle worth memorizing: when Christmas Day or New Year's Day lands on a Sunday, package stores also close the following Monday. That can mean a two-day gap right when the house is full of guests.</p>
