        <p>Other holidays (Fourth of July, Labor Day, Memorial Day, Christmas Eve, New Year's Eve) are normal business days for liquor stores under state law, and we're open our regular hours. We'll always post any holiday schedule on our <a href="/faq.html">FAQ page</a> and store doors.</p>

        <h2>Beer and Wine vs. Liquor Stores</h2>
        <p>This is where most of the confusion comes from. Texas treats beer and wine differently from distilled spirits, and different types of stores hold different permits.</p>
        <h3>Grocery and convenience stores</h3>
        <p>Beer and wine can be sold at grocery stores, convenience stores, and gas stations on Sundays starting at 10am. That Sunday start time moved earlier from noon in 2021. These stores cannot sell liquor at all, on any day.</p>
        <h3>Package stores like ours</h3>
        <p>A package store sells spirits, wine, and beer under one roof, six days a week. We're the only kind of retailer that can sell you a bottle of bourbon to take home. Learn more about what's on our shelves in our <a href="/guides/best-bourbon-whiskey-austin.html">bourbon guide</a> and <a href="/guides/texas-spirits-guide.html">Texas spirits guide</a>.</p>
        <h3>Bars and restaurants</h3>
        <p>On-premise businesses run under separate rules and can serve drinks on Sundays. That's why you can order a mimosa at brunch but can't buy a bottle of vodka on the way home.</p>

        <h2>Can I Buy Liquor on Sunday in Austin?</h2>
        <p>No. Liquor stores in Austin, and everywhere else in Texas, are closed on Sundays. You can buy beer and wine at grocery and convenience stores starting at 10am on Sunday, and bars and restaurants can serve you a drink. But if you want a bottle of tequila, whiskey, gin, or vodka to go, it has to be Monday through Saturday between 10am and 9pm.</p>

