        <h2>Alcohol Delivery Rules in Texas</h2>
        <p>Delivery from a liquor store is legal in Texas, but it comes with guardrails that we take seriously as a TABC-licensed, family-owned business:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A TABC delivery permit is required.</strong> Only permitted retailers can deliver alcohol.</li>
          <li><strong>The recipient must be 21 or older</strong> and show a valid government-issued photo ID at handoff.</li>
          <li><strong>No unattended drop-offs.</strong> We can't leave a bag on the porch, with a neighbor, or with a front desk. Someone 21+ has to receive it in person.</li>
          <li><strong>No handoff to anyone intoxicated.</strong> If the recipient appears intoxicated, the driver has to decline.</li>
          <li><strong>Undeliverable orders return to the store.</strong> If no one eligible is home, the order comes back.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Delivery follows store hours, so there's no Sunday delivery. For scope and terms, see our <a href="/delivery.html">delivery page</a>, and read our <a href="/guides/alcohol-delivery-austin-how-it-works.html">delivery how-to</a> for a step-by-step.</p>

        <h2>Curbside Pickup Rules</h2>
        <p>Curbside is the easy middle ground. Order online or call <a href="tel:5126144949">(512) 614-4949</a>, park out front, and call when you arrive. We bring the order to your car and check the ID of the person picking up. Same 21+ requirement, same store hours.</p>

        <h2>The Planning Tip Everyone Learns the Hard Way</h2>
        <p>If Sunday is the plan &mdash; game day, a lake afternoon, a Sunday-night dinner &mdash; shop on Saturday. Place an order online for delivery or curbside, or swing by either store before 9pm. It's a small habit that keeps the weekend running smoothly, and it's the reason our Saturday afternoons are the busiest hours of the week.</p>
        <p>When in doubt on any rule, TABC is the final word. We'll keep this guide current as the law changes, and the person behind the counter is always happy to explain what we can and can't do.</p>

        <h2>Common Questions</h2>
        <h3>What time do liquor stores open and close in Texas?</h3>
        <p>Package stores may sell from 10am to 9pm, Monday through Saturday. They are closed all day Sunday. Both Monarch locations keep exactly those hours.</p>
        <h3>Are liquor stores open on Christmas Eve or New Year's Eve in Texas?</h3>
        <p>Yes. State law only requires closure on Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Day, and New Year's Day. Christmas Eve and New Year's Eve are regular business days, though they're busy, so shop early.</p>
        <h3>Can someone else accept my alcohol delivery?</h3>
        <p>Any adult 21 or older at the address can accept it with a valid government photo ID. The driver can't leave it unattended or with anyone under 21.</p>
